# The `.work` language — a polyglot literate program

`.work` is the one thing you have no correct priors for. It is NOT markdown, NOT a single-language source
file, NOT a config format. It is a **literate program** in Knuth's sense — **prose and code woven into one
document you read top to bottom** — and it is **polyglot**: one file can host Rust, Zig, C, Python, JS and
more, each compiled to a confined WASM component. It is also the medium in which you **write applications**
and, for Autopoet, **rewrite your own body**.

**When to use:** ALWAYS, when authoring or editing `.work` — a user's app (external) or the agent's own
body (internal). Unlearn your markdown / HTML / one-file-one-language instincts first; they are wrong here.

## Literate, in Knuth's sense
The prose is NOT commentary around the code — it IS part of the program. The document narrates intent and
structure; `do … end` blocks are the runnable parts woven into that narrative. `Nexus.Literate` is the ONE
parser and reads the tree in order. **No HTML wrappers, no markdown code fences.** A block's first word is
its **kind**; the file reads as an essay whose examples execute. If the prose and the code disagree, the
file is wrong — they are one artifact.

## Polyglot — one file, many languages, all → WASM
A code block declares a language and compiles to a confined WASM component:
- **Compile lanes** (`nexus/lib/compile.ex`): `rust · zig · c · cpp · swift · python · js · ts`, plus
  framework lanes `svelte · solid`. Real WASM-native compilers ship in `nexus/compilers/`.
- `sandbox <lang> :name do … end` — a **capability-scoped** wrapper around an inner language; it routes to
  that language's lane and grants are enforced through the lane.
- **Placement is orthogonal to language:** `client :name` (browser WASM) vs `server :name` (BEAM / nexus);
  `def :name` is a runnable function; `resource :name do field … end` is a typed SQLite-backed table;
  `app` / `deploy` compose + ship.
- Polyglot does NOT mean native — everything untrusted runs in WASM (the runtime thesis): many *source*
  languages, one *confined* target.

## Two uses of the same language
1. **Writing applications** (external): structure an app as `.work` — prose narrates, `resource` declares
   data, `client` / `server` / `<lang>` blocks run, `app` / `deploy` compose + ship. One literate
   artifact, not a scattered repo.
2. **Rewriting your own code** (internal, Autopoet): the agent's body IS `.work`. It edits itself with
   `Autopoet.Body.write/2` — every write snapshotted to a reversible `history` (`undo` in both directions),
   so nothing is unrecoverable. The agent reads and rewrites the same literate medium it runs on — the
   self-improving loop. (See the internal `operate-your-body` skill.)

## The craft (why this is the highest-leverage skill)
- Read a `.work` file as a PROGRAM, top to bottom — the prose tells you what the blocks are for.
- Pick the **language per block** by the job (numeric/native → rust/c/zig; UI → js/ts/svelte/solid; data →
  `resource`); pick **placement** (`client`/`server`) by where it must run.
- Keep it literate: a `.work` file is an argument that runs. Author the narrative and the code together.
